{"id": "2025-09-01-cancer-abstracts-36pct-ai", "result": "unverifiable", "evidence": [{"doc": 2, "quote": "In aggregate, the presence of AI-generated text in 2024 varied greatly between abstracts (23%, 2749/11,959), methods text (10%, 1209/11,875), and reviewer comments (4.8%, 348/7211)."}, {"doc": 2, "quote": "In this cross-sectional study, original research manuscripts and reviewer comments submitted to 10 journals of the American Association for Cancer Research from 2021 through 2024 were analyzed for the presence of AI-generated text."}], "fetched": [{"doc": 1, "url": "https://www.science.org/content/article/far-more-authors-use-ai-write-science-papers-admit-it-publisher-reports", "status": 403, "ok": false, "chars": null, "fetched_at": "2026-10-12T21:34:21+00:00"}, {"doc": 2, "url": "https://peerreviewcongress.org/abstract/quantifying-and-assessing-the-use-of-generative-ai-by-authors-and-reviewers-in-the-cancer-research-field/", "status": 200, "ok": true, "chars": 4519, "fetched_at": "2026-10-12T21:42:42+00:00", "sha256": "4d460f31a4eccb8ae149d06ad2925d0198850c0477ff4c9e3956caff445343f0"}, {"doc": 3, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/d41586-025-02936-6", "status": 200, "ok": true, "chars": 235, "fetched_at": "2026-10-12T21:45:26+00:00", "sha256": "e32d22f5c92b1abae78d8498b576f3520068524ce7a00d9787b42dea2fe81dcc"}]}
{"id": "2025-09-01-maximum-effective-context-window", "result": "corrected", "evidence": [{"doc": 1, "quote": "Context Is What You Need: The Maximum Effective\nContext Window for Real World Limits of LLMs"}, {"doc": 1, "quote": "Large language model (LLM) providers boast big\nnumbers for maximum context window sizes. To test\nthe real-world use of context windows, we 1) define a\nconcept of maximum effective context window, 2)\nformulate a testing method of a context window’s\neffectiveness over various sizes and problem types"}, {"doc": 1, "quote": "A few top-of-the-line\nmodels in our test group failed with as few as 100\ntokens in context; most had severe degradation in\naccuracy by 1000 tokens in context. All models fell far\nshort of their Maximum Context Window by as much\nas >99%."}], "fetched": [{"doc": 1, "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2509.21361", "status": 200, "ok": true, "chars": 56354, "fetched_at": "2026-10-12T21:35:05+00:00", "sha256": "e2eaa711b58b13cd9e10065b071e5a52db9bfc18b71bbb54b5f54ae77b9b38fc"}, {"doc": 2, "url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20251216091737id_/https://arxiv.org/pdf/2509.21361", "status": 200, "ok": true, "chars": 55449, "fetched_at": "2026-10-12T21:36:06+00:00", "sha256": "5126d9666f5c694476083c9a2e47ffc578fbc25d71a3cd0a111c90cf0b04f458"}]}
{"id": "2025-09-01-openai-why-models-hallucinate", "result": "verified", "evidence": [{"doc": 3, "quote": "September 5, 2025\nResearchPublication\nWhy language models hallucinate"}, {"doc": 3, "quote": "standard training and evaluation procedures reward guessing over acknowledging uncertainty."}, {"doc": 3, "quote": "Metric\ngpt-5-thinking-mini\nOpenAI o4-mini\nAbstention rate\n(no specific answer is given)\n52%\n1%\nAccuracy rate\n(right answer, higher is better)\n22%\n24%\nError rate\n(wrong answer, lower is better)\n26%\n75%"}, {"doc": 3, "quote": "Most scoreboards prioritize and rank models based on accuracy, but errors are worse than abstentions."}], "fetched": [{"doc": 1, "url": "https://openai.com/index/why-language-models-hallucinate/", "status": 403, "ok": false, "chars": null, "fetched_at": "2026-10-12T21:36:06+00:00"}, {"doc": 2, "url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20260106221040id_/https://openai.com/index/why-language-models-hallucinate/", "status": 200, "ok": true, "chars": 11915, "fetched_at": "2026-10-12T21:37:56+00:00", "sha256": "b64a2de4739c9da032026c192e74f9adbc2b40fa5c52253aefd5d11cf00e4723"}, {"doc": 3, "url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20250923153111id_/https://openai.com/index/why-language-models-hallucinate/", "status": 200, "ok": true, "chars": 11954, "fetched_at": "2026-10-12T21:38:02+00:00", "sha256": "c772f21ab8c50dcc46b0629a91cee9efee35793eed31e9d08f2ed8e63593283c"}]}
